--- body ---
## Context

A PR body's `**Ticket:**` line is executed by `erg-pr-merge`, not by the forge.
The extraction regex matches `tickets/NNNN` and not `tickets/closed/NNNN`, so
when a ticket is archived before the merge the close claim is skipped with no
output and a non-zero exit nowhere. `erg check` passes either way, because the
store is internally consistent -- the ticket is simply still open while its fix
sits on main.

This is the mechanism behind git-erg PR #334 (2026-09-10): ticket 0276's fix
landed while 0276 stayed open, found by luck in a `/perch` pass and repaired by
a second PR.

The defect has two live instances, which is why it is filed as a class rather
than a one-line fix:

- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge:146` (this repo) --
  `grep -oiP '^\*{0,2}ticket:?\*{0,2}:?\s*tickets/\K\d+'`
- `tickets/erg-github:77` (git-erg) --
  `grep -iE '^[*]{0,2}ticket:?[*]{0,2}:? *tickets/[0-9]{4}'`, covered there by
  **git-erg ticket 0255**, salvaged onto main 2026-09-16 in git-erg PR #337.
- `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh:109` (this repo) -- byte-identical to the
  `erg-pr-merge` copy.

That third one is the worst of the set, because it is the **detector**. A body
carrying `tickets/closed/NNNN` fails ID extraction at line 109, then matches the
`Ticket: none | tickets/` classifier at line 115, so the PR is counted as an
*explicit no-close* and never reported. The detector is blind to precisely the
case the bug produces, and its all-clear is indistinguishable from "I could not
look" -- the trap `rules/git.md` keeps naming.

Verified 2026-09-16 with a positive control, not by reading:

```
body: **Ticket:** tickets/0276-some-slug.erg        -> extracts 0276
body: **Ticket:** tickets/closed/0276-some-slug.erg -> extracts nothing,
                                                       and matches the
                                                       no-close classifier
```

Consequence for past runs: a `check-close-claims.sh` report of "0 findings, N
explicit no-close" cannot be trusted to mean no claim was dropped. The "explicit
no-close" bucket silently absorbs them.

The harness copy is the one that runs at most merges, so fixing only the git-erg
side leaves the common path broken.

## Second defect in the same parser: it is fence-blind

The extraction is a line-oriented `grep`, so it does not know about Markdown
code fences. **A PR body that documents the claim syntax carries live claims.**

Observed on this ticket's own paper trail: PR #917's body contains, inside a
fenced block illustrating the bug, a line reading
`**Ticket:** tickets/0276-some-slug.erg` followed by its expected output. That
line matches the *current* regex. #917 was merged with a plain `gh pr merge`, so
nothing fired; through `erg-pr-merge` the claim on 0276 would have been
extracted. Harness 0276 is archived, so step 1.5's existence-at-tip guard would
have aborted the merge -- confusing, but safe. Had the illustrative ID named an
**open** ticket, existence-at-tip passes and the ticket closes spuriously.

This interacts with the main fix rather than being independent of it: widening
the grammar to accept the closed path makes a second illustrative line
extractable too, and requires step 1.5 to look in `tickets/closed/` as well, or
every legitimate closed-path claim will abort.

Options, none yet chosen -- a design call for whoever takes the ticket: strip
fenced blocks before matching; require the claim outside any fence; or keep the
parser dumb and add a lint that refuses a PR body whose fenced content would
match. The last is the cheapest and the most honest about what the parser is.


## Relevant files

- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` -- line 146, `TICKET_IDS` extraction
- `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh` -- the after-the-fact detector; it joins
  from the merged-PR side and is what would catch a regression in the field
- git-erg `tickets/erg-github` -- the sibling copy, fixed under git-erg 0255

## Actions

1. Widen the extraction to accept an optional `closed/` segment, keeping the
   captured group the bare 4-digit ID.
2. Confirm `erg close` on an already-archived ticket is a no-op or a clean
   success, so that accepting the path does not turn a skipped claim into a
   hard failure at the merge gate.
3. Fix `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh:109` in the same pass, and make the
   line-115 classifier stop swallowing a closed-path claim into "explicit
   no-close" -- a claim it cannot parse must be reported, not counted as
   deliberate.
4. Coordinate with git-erg 0255 so all copies end up with the same accepted
   grammar; a divergence here is worse than the bug, because the paths would
   then disagree about which PR bodies are valid.

## What git-erg 0255's landing established (2026-09-16)

The sibling fix shipped. It took **three review rounds, and the first two were
worse than the bug.** Read this before touching the regex here: the harness can
buy the result without repaying the rounds.

**Failure 1 — widening only the grep.** Adding `closed/` to the `grep` filter
and not to the paired `sed` left the two stages disagreeing about case: `grep
-i` folds every literal, a BRE `sed` folds none. A mis-cased `tickets/Closed/`
cleared the filter, failed to substitute, and passed the whole body line
through as the id. Ugly, and a hard failure — but fail-closed.

**Failure 2 — guarding the symptom.** The repair added a trailing
`grep -xE '[0-9]{4}'` to discard the unsubstituted line. That removed the leak
and **converted fail-closed into fail-open**: an empty id list *is* the escape
hatch, so an open ticket named `TICKETS/0043` returned exit 0 and passed the
required gate. The PR's own test for the case asserted `rc==0` and no leak,
which both the intended resolution and the escape hatch satisfy, so it passed
while proving nothing.

**What worked.** Case-fold the body once, up front (`tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'`)
and drop `grep -i`, so filter and substitution cannot disagree about any
literal — the one added later included. Adopt that grammar here verbatim;
Action 4 requires the copies to agree, and this is now the thing to agree with.

**The general shape, which is the part worth keeping.** In a parser feeding a
gate, *"I could not read it"* and *"there is nothing to read"* land in the same
branch, and that branch is the permissive one. Widening what the parser attempts
does not touch that confusion — it enlarges the set of inputs that can fall into
it. This is why Action 3's second half (report an unparseable claim rather than
counting it as deliberate) is not a tidiness item: it is what stops the next
widening from becoming the next bypass.

## The harness has a coupling git-erg did not

`tickets/erg-github` only *reads* state. `erg-pr-merge` *mutates*: it closes,
commits, pushes, merges. Two consequences, the first verified in the current
script on 2026-09-16:

- **Existence-at-tip globs `tickets/` alone.** Line ~210 is
  `compgen -G "tickets/${tid}-*.erg"`. An archived ticket is not there, so a
  successfully-extracted closed-path id is reported MISSING and the script
  **dies** — unless `ERG_PR_MERGE_ALLOW_MISSING_TICKET=1`. Widening only the
  regex therefore moves the failure from "die: no close-claim in PR body" to
  "die: ticket absent from tickets/". Different message, same hard stop, and a
  superficial test of the regex would report success.
- **`erg close` on an already-archived ticket** must be a clean no-op, which is
  Action 2 — but note it is reached only after the guard above is fixed, so the
  two cannot be sequenced independently.

So this is three coupled changes, not one: the grammar, the existence-at-tip
lookup, and the already-closed close. Any one alone leaves the path broken in a
way that looks fixed.

## Evidence that the trap is live and recurring

Three firings on 2026-09-16, two of them by the agent that had just documented
it:

- git-erg PR #340's body was written with a `tickets/closed/0281-...` claim;
  caught before merge and rewritten to `Ticket: none`.
- Harness PR #929's body, same mistake, same day, after the above.
- The original report (git-erg 0255) came from aedist PR #442/#443.

The practical workaround until this lands stays as recorded: do not pre-close a
ticket before running the merge, or write `Ticket: none` when the close is
already in the diff.

## Test

Red step first: a unit test that feeds a PR body carrying
`**Ticket:** tickets/closed/0276-some-slug.erg` to the extraction and asserts it
yields `0276`. It must fail against the current line 146 before any fix lands --
a green-on-arrival test here proves nothing, since the open-path case already
passes.

## Verification

- [ ] The red test above fails before the change and passes after
- [ ] `tickets/NNNN`, `**Ticket:** tickets/NNNN` and `Ticket: tickets/NNNN` all
      still extract, including the multi-line `sort -u` case
- [ ] A body with no ticket line still yields empty without error
- [ ] `check-close-claims.sh --days 7` still reports 0 findings

## Implementation decision and remaining verification (2026-09-23)

Markdown fenced blocks are stripped before interpreting Ticket lines in both
the merge gate and detector. This keeps examples from closing tickets. The
scripts fold case before matching the same `tickets/(closed/)?NNNN` path forms;
the gate rejects and the detector reports any Ticket path it cannot parse.
Regression fixtures cover a closed-path claim, mixed-case claim, malformed
claim next to `Ticket: none`, and a fenced example. The pre-change extraction
regex returns no ID for `tickets/closed/0276-some-slug.erg`; the new integration
fixture closes the case through the full merge script.

The live `check-close-claims.sh --days 30 --limit 100` attempt exited 2:
`gh pr list` returned no data in this environment. A before/after comparison
and the 7-day all-clear cannot be asserted until forge access works. The
git-erg sibling's source was also unavailable here; its landing is described
in the ticket log, but exact source parity remains to be checked.

Both targeted shell suites pass, and `erg validate` passes for this ticket.
`make check` reached pytest and reported unrelated failures in the broader
shell suite, including `test_bash_env_xtrace_silent.sh` (its fixture did not
load); the full run was stopped after that failure was reproduced alone.

## Invariants

- The `harness-extension-point` comments around the forge-specific calls stay
  where they are; this change is not a refactor of that boundary.
- Do not change what the script does when extraction yields empty -- that path
  is the "explicit no-close" PR, which is legitimate and common.

## Exit criteria

- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` and `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh` both
  extract a ticket ID from `tickets/NNNN` and `tickets/closed/NNNN` alike
- `check-close-claims.sh` reports, rather than silently absorbing, a claim whose
  path form it cannot parse
- A re-run of `check-close-claims.sh --days 30` after the fix is compared
  against the pre-fix run, since the pre-fix "explicit no-close" counts may hide
  real drops
- A decision is recorded on the fence-blind defect above, and step 1.5's
  existence-at-tip guard accepts `tickets/closed/` once the grammar does
- The red test is committed and passes
- The accepted grammar is the same in this repo and in git-erg's
  `tickets/erg-github`, or the difference is documented in both
